//? Title: Quine-McCluskey: actual prime rounds, coverage chart and exact minimum cover
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A Boolean truth table can require many minterms even when a short sum of
products exists. Quine-McCluskey merges adjacent cubes into prime implicants,
finds essential primes and solves the remaining cover exactly. Actual native
merge rounds and the coverage chart now explain the reduction. The original
four-variable function and two dont-cares remain, alongside zero-variable and
duplicate-domain controls.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

Represent a product by fixed bits and a dash mask. Terms with equal masks and
exactly one differing fixed bit merge by replacing that bit with a dash. The
merged cube is precisely their union, so it covers only required or permitted
assignments. A term never merged in its round is prime; iterating exhausts all
such cubes.
$$ \bigwedge_{m\in M}\bigvee_{p:\,p\text{ covers }m}z_p. $$
\alttext(For every required minterm at least one selected covering prime must be present.)
A chart column with one covering prime forces an essential prime. After
essentials cover their columns, native Petrick expansion enumerates irredundant
residual products and selects minimum cardinality. Among those covers the
producer minimizes total fixed literals. This gives a lexicographic objective:
fewest product terms first, then fewest literals, not unrestricted literal
minimization. The worst-case search is exponential; independent term, pair,
chart and covering budgets prevent treating small examples as a scalable
guarantee.

